1. Nf3 c5
2. e4 Nc6
3. d4 cxd4
4. Nxd4 Nf6
5. Nc3 e6
6. Nxc6 bxc6
7. e5 Nd5
8. Ne4 Qc7
9. f4
{The Exchange/Four-Knights reminder: after `Nxc6` and `e5`, use the e4 square and play around Black's damaged c-pawns.} *

[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - Maroczy Bind"]
[Site "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YzI6qI-33_U"]
[Date "2023.03.06"]
[Round "5"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Accelerated / Hyperaccelerated Dragon"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "GM Daniel Naroditsky video"]
[Orientation "white"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Accelerated Dragon, Maroczy Bind"]
[SourceTitle "Understanding the Accelerated Dragon!! | Open Sicilian | GM Naroditsky's Theory Speed Run"]
[SourceURL "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YzI6qI-33_U"]

1. Nf3 c5
2. e4 Nc6
3. d4 cxd4
4. Nxd4 g6
(4... d6
5. c4 g6
6. Be3 Bg7
7. Nc3 Nf6
8. Be2 O-O
9. O-O Bd7
10. Qd2
{Same weak-deck Maroczy shell against the `...d6` move order.})
5. c4
{Naroditsky notes that strong players usually choose the Maroczy Bind against the Accelerated Dragon. The point is to clamp down on `...d5`.}
5... Bg7
6. Be3 Nf6
7. Nc3 O-O
8. Be2 d6
9. O-O Bd7
10. Qd2
{Connect rooks, support Bh6 ideas, and prepare Rfd1 or Rac1. The bind is about control, not a quick mate.} *

[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - Najdorf and Dragon Branches"]
[Site "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zEqoGIgzk1E"]
[Date "2023.07.15"]
[Round "6"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Najdorf / Dragon"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "GM Daniel Naroditsky videos"]
[Orientation "white"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Najdorf and Dragon"]
[SourceTitle "Unveiling the Strategy | Sicilian Najdorf | GM Naroditsky's Theory Speed Run; Dragon Masterclass"]
[SourceURL "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zEqoGIgzk1E"]
[SecondarySourceURL "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kYbh2NTFsS8"]

1. Nf3 c5
2. e4 d6
3. d4 cxd4
4. Nxd4 Nf6
5. Nc3 a6
(5... g6
{Dragon branch from the weak deck.}
6. Be2 Bg7
7. O-O O-O
8. Be3 Nc6
9. Qd2
{The calmer Dragon setup: castle short, develop smoothly, then decide how sharp you want the game to become.})
6. Be3
{English Attack shell. Naroditsky explains that `...a6` supports `...e5`, `...b5`, and many later queenside ideas; your setup must be purposeful.}
6... e6
7. f3 Be7
8. Qd2 O-O
9. O-O-O
{If Black castles short, your plan is Kb1, g4, h4, and kingside pressure while respecting `...b5` counterplay.} *

[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - Modern Sicilian and Marshall Reminder"]
[Site "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ihv2TK04WPg"]
[Date "2021.06.21"]
[Round "7"]
[White "White repertoire"]
[Black "Modern / Marshall"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "GM Daniel Naroditsky video"]
[Orientation "white"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Miscellaneous"]

1. e4 c5
2. Nf3 d6
(2... e6
3. d4 d5
4. exd5 exd5
5. Bb5+
{Marshall Counterattack reminder. Give the check, develop quickly, and do not let Black get a comfortable IQP position for free.}
5... Nc6
6. O-O Be7
7. Re1 Nf6)
3. d4 cxd4
4. Nxd4 Nf6
5. Bd3
{Modern/Scheveningen-style weak-deck branch. `Bd3` supports e4 and prepares a calmer kingside setup.}
5... g6
6. O-O Bg7
7. c3 O-O
8. Re1 Nc6
{Sicilian summary: `Bd3` mostly belongs against e6/Kan/Scheveningen structures; `Nc3` is the default against Najdorf and most `...d6` systems; `c4` is your Maroczy answer to Dragon fianchetto systems; `Bg5` versus Sveshnikov aims for d5 control.} *
